# Code owners (`08` §3.15, `11` §3.8.3, `13` §3.F.1).
#
# This file says WHO reviews. Branch protection says HOW MANY.
#
# **Nothing here is enforced today.** Ruleset `main` has `required_approving_review_count: 0` AND
# `require_code_owner_review: false`, so this file is documentation until there is a second
# maintainer. Both settings are off for the same unavoidable reason and neither is a shortcut:
# GitHub does not let an author approve their own pull request, so requiring either an approval or
# a code-owner approval would block every change on a one-maintainer project.
#
# The M0 independent review was right to flag that `require_code_owner_review: false` had not been
# disclosed — the M0-T17 tick said "CODEOWNERS over every path `08` §3.15 protects", which is true
# of the file and silent about enforcement. It is stated here, in `docs/architecture/ci.md` and in
# STATUS now.
#
# The paths below are the ones `08` §3.15 marks as needing **two** maintainer approvals. Writing
# them out correctly now means turning enforcement on is one settings change rather than an
# archaeology exercise.
#
# Last match wins, so the protected paths are at the bottom.

# Everything, by default.
*                                       @faizannraza

# --- Protected: a mistake here means somebody's private message reaches the wrong person --------
# Information flow and policy. These decide who may know what and who may do what.
/packages/core/src/ifc/                 @faizannraza
/packages/core/src/policy/              @faizannraza

# Context building, the outbound guard and the declassifier: the three places where a private item
# can escape into a reply.
/packages/engine/src/context/           @faizannraza
/packages/engine/src/guard/             @faizannraza
/packages/engine/src/declassifier/      @faizannraza
/packages/engine/src/tools-executor/    @faizannraza

